window.QUESTION_WORKED = {"cie-9701-2021-mj-p12-q33": {"answer": "C", "solution": "1 is FALSE. Dynamic equilibrium requires a closed system in which the forward and reverse reactions keep happening at the same rate. Here NH₄Cl is heated in an open tube: the NH₃ and HCl gases travel away and condense on the cold surface, so the products are continuously removed.\n2 is TRUE. The NH₃ and HCl can react again to reform NH₄Cl, so the reaction is reversible.\n3 is TRUE. Heat has to be supplied to decompose NH₄Cl, so the forward reaction is endothermic.\n→ 2 and 3 only = C"}, "cie-9701-2024-on-p11-q12": {"answer": "A", "solution": "The middle vessel holds N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ at equilibrium. The right-hand vessel contains lithium, which reacts with nitrogen to form Li₃N (it removes N₂). The left-hand vessel is evacuated.\nIf tap 2 is opened, lithium removes N₂, so [N₂] falls → the equilibrium shifts LEFT (to make more N₂) → less NH₃.\nIf tap 1 is opened, the gas spreads into the evacuated vessel, so the volume increases and the pressure falls → the equilibrium shifts to the side with MORE gas moles (left: 4 mol vs 2 mol) → less NH₃.\n→ To keep the most ammonia, leave both taps closed = A"}, "cie-9701-2021-mj-p13-q11": {"answer": "D", "solution": "4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) ⇌ 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g)    ΔH = −1636 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic)\nGas moles: 9 on the left → 10 on the right (more moles of product).\nTemperature: the forward reaction is exothermic, so a LOW temperature favours the products and increases the yield of NO.\nPressure: the product side has MORE gas moles, so a LOW pressure favours the products.\n→ low temperature + low pressure = D"}, "cie-9701-2023-on-p13-q12": {"answer": "B", "solution": "2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)    ΔH = −57 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic)\nGas moles: 2 on the left → 1 on the right (fewer moles of product).\nTemperature: exothermic, so a LOW temperature favours N₂O₄.\nPressure: the product side has FEWER gas moles, so a HIGH pressure favours N₂O₄.\n→ high pressure + low temperature = B"}, "cie-9701-2023-fm-p12-q5": {"answer": "D", "solution": "2NOCl(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) + Cl₂(g)    ΔH = +77 kJ mol⁻¹ (endothermic)\nOnly a change in TEMPERATURE changes the value of the equilibrium constant, K (concentration, pressure and catalysts move the position of equilibrium but do NOT change K).\nFor an endothermic reaction, increasing the temperature favours the forward reaction, so the equilibrium shifts to the RIGHT and K increases.\n→ an increase in temperature = D"}, "cie-9701-2021-on-p11-q10": {"answer": "B", "solution": "H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)    ΔH = −9.6 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic)\nAt a lower temperature T₂, the exothermic forward reaction is favoured, so the equilibrium shifts to the RIGHT: more HI and LESS I₂. The equilibrium mole fraction of I₂ is therefore LOWER than 0.23.\nA lower temperature also makes the reaction slower, so the curve takes LONGER to reach its equilibrium value.\n→ graph B (slower approach, lower final mole fraction of I₂) = B"}, "cie-9701-2024-fm-p12-q14": {"answer": "D", "solution": "H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g), with inert argon added at constant volume and constant temperature.\nArgon does not react, and at constant volume its addition does not change the moles (or the concentrations) of H₂, I₂ and HI. Each partial pressure depends only on its own moles and the volume, so the partial pressure of HI is unchanged.\nThe rate of the forward reaction depends on the concentrations of H₂ and I₂, which are unchanged.\n(There are equal gas moles on both sides, 2 ⇌ 2, so the position of equilibrium does not shift anyway.)\n→ rate unaffected, partial pressure of HI unaffected = D"}};
